  "worked_solution": "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}^{2} & >5{x}^{2}+14 \\\\ 7{x}^{2}-5{x}^{2} & >14 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & >14 \\\\ {x}^{2} & >7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & >7 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{7} \\\\ |x| & >\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have \n\n$$\nx<-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\sqrt{7}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{7})\\cup (\\sqrt{7},\\infty ).$\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from ${x}^{2}>7$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-7>0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $y={x}^{2}-7$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-7 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =7 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x=\\pm \\sqrt{7}.$ \n\nSince the leading coefficient of ${x}^{2}-7$ is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, the solution to the given inequality ($y>0$) lies outside the roots (but does not include them).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/1eba3bc41624ea6fdd11468b53630fd8995541ff01548ec57a36dad271e375f8.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{7})\\cup (\\sqrt{7},\\infty ).$",
